\description{
Documents are drawn with replacement, and \code{\link[=getMeasures]{getMeasures()}} is run again on
each resample. A document drawn twice counts as two documents.
}
\details{
A combination that does not occur in a resample is left out of that
resample, instead of being counted as zero. This is intended: a real sample
can also miss rare combinations, so the interval of each combination
describes its measures given that it was observed. \code{presence} gives the
proportion of resamples in which each combination was observed.
Replicates where a measure is \code{NA} or \code{NaN} are also left out, so
\code{n_resamples} in \code{intervals} can be lower than \code{n_present}.

Interval types:
\itemize{
\item \code{"perc"}: percentile interval.
\item \code{"bca"}: bias-corrected and accelerated interval. The acceleration comes
from a leave-one-document-out jackknife on the original data, which costs
one more \code{\link[=getMeasures]{getMeasures()}} call per document. The bias correction counts ties
with the original value as half, which matters for discrete measures and
for counts in \code{statistics}. The interval is \code{NA} when the original value is
below or above every resample (the bias correction is infinite then). When
all jackknife values are equal, the acceleration is taken as 0.
\item \code{"stud"}: studentized (bootstrap-t) interval,
\eqn{[\hat\theta - t^*_{1-\alpha/2} \hat{se}, \hat\theta - t^*_{\alpha/2} \hat{se}]},
where \eqn{t^* = (\theta^* - \hat\theta) / se^*} for each resample. With
\code{inner = "jackknife"}, \eqn{se^*} is a leave-one-document-out jackknife on
the resample and \eqn{\hat{se}} the same jackknife on the original data
(\code{se_jack}). This costs one \code{\link[=getMeasures]{getMeasures()}} call per distinct document
in each resample: leaving out either copy of a document drawn twice gives
the same result, so it is computed once and weighted twice. With
\code{inner = "bootstrap"}, \eqn{se^*} is the SD over \code{R_inner} resamples of the
resample and \eqn{\hat{se}} the SD over the outer resamples (\code{se}). This costs
\code{R_inner} calls per resample. Resamples where \eqn{se^*} is 0 or missing give no
\eqn{t^*} and are left out (\code{n_stud} counts the rest). This happens often for
discrete measures.
}

In both jackknifes, a combination that disappears when a document is left
out is left out of that jackknife sample, and the jackknife uses the
remaining samples.

With \code{freq_col}, \code{data} can be a table of frequencies, which gives the same
results as the tokens it was counted from (under the same seed). With
\code{keep_zero_rows = TRUE}, a combination listed with frequency 0 in a document
stays in every resample that draws that document, with \code{n = 0}, instead of
being dropped as unobserved.
}
